{"delta": {"role": "assistant"}, "context": {"thoughts": [{"title": "Prompt to generate search query", "description": [{"role": "system", "content": "Below is a history of the conversation so far, and a new message from the volunteer. The volunteer is entering the applicant's situation or follow-up answers to be searched in the legal knowledge base.\nYou have access to an Azure AI Search index containing legal guidance, triage questions, and routing information.\nGenerate a search query based on the conversation and the new message. The query should capture the legal issue or situation so that the right Golden Set entry (Part A + Part B) can be retrieved.\nTreat the newest volunteer message as the primary source of truth for topic selection.\nIf the newest message indicates a different legal issue from earlier turns, ignore earlier-turn legal terms entirely and build the query from the newest message only.\nDo not carry over nouns, institutions, statutes, or issue labels from previous turns unless the newest message clearly refers to the same matter.\nIf the newest message looks like a short follow-up answer to a prior triage question (for example \"yes\", \"no\", a date, an amount, or a brief fact update), preserve the same legal topic from recent turns and include topic terms from that ongoing issue in the search query.\nWhen the conversation already has a stable selected Golden Set entry/topic and the newest message is an answer-only update, keep retrieval anchored to that same entry/topic instead of switching domains.\nPrefer terms that match how entries are written in the index: **topic**, **category**, and distinctive phrases similar to **`user_query`** / **`variations`** in the knowledge base. Include a **Golden Set code** (e.g. EMP-01, FAM-03, BKR-02) only when the topic clearly maps to that bucket—codes appear in indexed JSON and filenames such as `EMP-01.json`.\nWhen the volunteer’s wording is colloquial, **carry over their concrete nouns and verbs** (e.g. who, what happened, which institution) into the search query; do not replace everything with generic words.\nUse the correct **legal domain** keywords for the issue (e.g. family law vs employment vs bankruptcy) so the query is not dominated by the wrong area.\nIf the volunteer asks about eligibility or routing through legal aid programs, include relevant program terms (for example legal aid eligibility, PCHI, savings threshold, civil/criminal/family routing, LAB, CLAS, PDO, LASCO, FJSS).\nDo not paste long file paths or irrelevant document titles into the search query. **Golden Set entry codes** (e.g. EMP-01, FAM-03) are allowed when they match the legal issue and improve retrieval.\nDo not include any text inside [] or <<>> in the search query terms.\nDo not include any special characters like '+'.\nAssume all messages are in English.\nWhen uncertain between history and latest message, prefer the latest message and output a concise, neutral query without a Golden Set code.\nIf you cannot generate a search query, return just the number 0.\n\nGenerate search query for: My employer hasn't paid my salary for 3 months. What can I do?\n\nSearch query: Unpaid salary employer not paid wages claim TADM Employment Act\n\nGenerate search query for: Applicant was fired without reason last week.\n\nSearch query: Wrongful dismissal unfair termination TADM Employment Act\n\nGenerate search query for: My ex won't let me see my kids.\n\nSearch query: FAM-03 child access denied visitation custody ex-spouse co-parent Family Law care and control joint custody FJC children\n\nGenerate search query for: I need help dividing my late parent's estate among the siblings.\n\nSearch query: Probate estate administration will intestacy beneficiaries succession Wills\n\n\nGenerate search query for: Are interest rates high?"}], "props": {"model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "deployment": "test-chatgpt", "token_usage": {"prompt_tokens": 23, "completion_tokens": 896, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "total_tokens": 919}}}, {"title": "Search using generated search query", "description": "interest rates", "props": {"use_semantic_captions": false, "use_semantic_ranker": false, "use_query_rewriting": false, "use_azure_search_query_rewriting": false, "top": 3, "filter": null, "use_vector_search": true, "use_text_search": true, "search_text_embeddings": true, "search_image_embeddings": true, "rewrite_fallback_to_user_query": false, "minimum_search_score": 0.0, "minimum_reranker_score": 0.0}}, {"title": "Search results", "description": [{"type": "searchIndex", "id": "file-Financial_Market_Analysis_Report_2023_pdf-46696E616E6369616C204D61726B657420416E616C79736973205265706F727420323032332E706466-page-7", "content": " This\nsection examines the correlations between stock indices, cryptocurrency prices, and commodity prices,\nrevealing how changes in one market can have ripple effects across the financial ecosystem.### Impact of Macroeconomic Factors\n\n\n<figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>\n\n\nMacroeconomic factors such as interest\nrates, inflation, and GDP growth play a\npivotal role in shaping financial markets.", "category": null, "sourcepage": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7", "sourcefile": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf", "oids": null, "groups": null, "captions": [], "score": null, "reranker_score": null, "activity": null, "images": []}, {"type": "searchIndex", "id": "file-Financial_Market_Analysis_Report_2023_pdf-46696E616E6369616C204D61726B657420416E616C79736973205265706F727420323032332E706466-page-8", "content": "</figcaption></figure>\n\n\nMacroeconomic factors such as interest\nrates, inflation, and GDP growth play a\npivotal role in shaping financial markets.\nThis section analyzes how these factors\nhave influenced stock, cryptocurrency,\nand commodity markets over recent\nyears, providing insights into the\ncomplex relationship between the\neconomy and financial market\nperformance.## Future Predictions and Trends\n\n\n<figure><figcaption>Relative Growth Trends for S&P 500, Bitcoin, and Oil Prices (2024 Indexed to 100)<br>This horizontal bar chart shows prices indexed to 100 for the years 2024 to 2028. It compares the prices of Oil, Bitcoin, and the S&P 500 across these years. In 2024, all three have an index value of 100. From 2025 to 2028, all three generally increase, with Bitcoin consistently having the highest index value, followed closely by the S&P 500 and then Oil. The chart uses grey bars for Oil, orange bars for Bitcoin, and blue bars for the S&P 500.</figcaption></figure>\n\n\nBased on historical data, current trends,\nand economic indicators, this section\npresents predictions for the future of\nfinancial markets.", "category": null, "sourcepage": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8", "sourcefile": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf", "oids": null, "groups": null, "captions": [], "score": null, "reranker_score": null, "activity": null, "images": [{"url": "https://sticygqdubf4x6w.blob.core.windows.net/images/Financial%20Market%20Analysis%20Report%202023.pdf/page7/figure8_1.png", "description": "<figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>", "boundingbox": [63.1008, 187.9416, 561.3408000000001, 483.5088]}]}, {"type": "searchIndex", "id": "file-Financial_Market_Analysis_Report_2023_pdf-46696E616E6369616C204D61726B657420416E616C79736973205265706F727420323032332E706466-page-1", "content": "advanced data\nanalytics to present a clear picture of the complex interplay between\ndifferent financial markets and their potential trajectories## Introduction to Financial Markets\n\n\n<figure><figcaption>Global Financial Market Distribution (2023)<br>The pie chart features four categories: Stocks, Bonds, Cryptocurrencies, and Commodities. Stocks take up the largest portion of the chart, represented in blue, accounting for 40%. Bonds are the second largest, shown in orange, making up 25%. Cryptocurrencies are depicted in gray and cover 20% of the chart. Commodities are the smallest segment, shown in yellow, comprising 15%.</figcaption></figure>\n\n\nThe global financial market is a vast and intricate network of\nexchanges, instruments, and assets, ranging from traditional stocks\nand bonds to modern cryptocurrencies and commodities. Each\nsegment plays a crucial role in the overall economy, and their\ninteractions can have profound effects on global financial stability.\nThis section provides an overview of these segments and sets the\nstage for a detailed analysis## Stock Market Overview\n\n\n<figure><figcaption><br>The image is a line graph titled \"5-Year Trend of the S&P 500 Index.", "category": null, "sourcepage": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2", "sourcefile": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf", "oids": null, "groups": null, "captions": [], "score": null, "reranker_score": null, "activity": null, "images": [{"url": "https://sticygqdubf4x6w.blob.core.windows.net/images/Financial%20Market%20Analysis%20Report%202023.pdf/page7/figure8_1.png", "description": "<figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>", "boundingbox": [63.1008, 187.9416, 561.3408000000001, 483.5088]}]}], "props": null}, {"title": "Prompt to generate answer", "description": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a Legal Triage Assistant for Pro Bono SG hotline interns and volunteers. The intern has ZERO legal knowledge — you are their expert guide. A applicant is sitting right in front of the intern, so every response must be SHORT, ACTIONABLE, and SCANNABLE IN UNDER 15 SECONDS. No walls of text. No essays.\n\nYour job: read the intern's description, match it to the right Golden Set entry, figure out what you already know from their paragraph, and tell the intern exactly what to do next — either ask ONE specific follow-up question or route the applicant immediately.\n\n---\n\nSTEP 1 — TOPIC MATCH (mandatory first step)\n\n- Read the intern's description of the applicant's situation.\n- **Scan every source block below** for Golden Set JSON (look for `\"id\":` such as `GEN3-T01`, plus `category`, `topic`, `user_query`, `variations`, and `guardrail`). Do **not** decide from the first snippet or ranking order alone — irrelevant entries may rank higher.\n- **Strong match rule:** If the intern's message clearly paraphrases any entry's `user_query` or `variations`, treat that entry as the primary match when its full JSON appears in the sources.\n- **Pick the one entry** whose legal area and issue match the situation.\n- **Never invent an entry id.** `Selected Entry` must be an id visible in the sources (or Unclear).\n- If **no entry matches after scanning all sources**, use **Unclear** handling (OUTPUT D below).\n\nENTRY CONSISTENCY (apply in every response)\n- Citations may show **sourcefile** `GEN3-T01.json` (one file per Golden Set row). When the basename matches `\"id\"` in the JSON body, treat that as a strong signal.\n- The sources may contain multiple entries. Pick the **single best-matching** one across all chunks.\n- Use ONLY that entry's triage questions, branching logic, routes, and guardrail. Do not merge across entries unless the entry explicitly cross-references another.\n- **Guardrail adherence (non-negotiable):** The `guardrail` field is binding. Never paraphrase away restrictions. If the guardrail forbids something (e.g. interpreting documents, applying law to facts), refuse that part.\n- If no clear match exists after scanning all sources, use Unclear handling.\n\n---\n\nSTEP 2 — AUTO-EXTRACT (the critical innovation)\n\nAfter matching a topic, read the intern's paragraph and check EVERY Part B question from `triage_questions` and `branching_logic` in that entry:\n\n- For each question, determine if the intern's paragraph **already provides the answer** — either explicitly stated or clearly implied.\n- Be conservative: only mark a question as answered if the paragraph **unambiguously** provides the answer. If ambiguous, treat as unanswered.\n- Follow the `branching_logic` with the answered questions to determine how far down the decision tree you can get.\n- Detect cross-references: if the description reveals urgency cues (violence, self-harm, court date within 14 days, homelessness), flag GEN3-T06 concurrently. If vulnerability cues appear (elderly, minor, language barrier, disability), flag GEN3-T13 concurrently.\n\n---\n\nSTEP 3 — RESPOND (use the right output format)\n\n**OUTPUT A — Can route immediately (all necessary questions answered or branching logic reaches a route)**\n\n**Selected Entry:** <ENTRY_ID>\n\n**What I gathered from your description:**\n- Q<n>: <question summary> → <extracted answer> (1 line per answered question)\n\n**Part C — Routing recommendation:** Route <letter> (<name>)\n\nTell the applicant: **\"<exact script the intern reads aloud to the applicant — 2-3 sentences max, plain language, no legal jargon>\"**\n\nNext steps for you (the intern):\n- <1-2 bullet points of what the intern should do, e.g. \"Take down applicant's name and contact\" or \"Transfer to PBSG Staff\">\n\nCite [filename] for every fact.\n\n**OUTPUT B — Need more info (some questions answered, more needed before routing)**\n\n**Selected Entry:** <ENTRY_ID>\n\n**What I gathered from your description:**\n- Q<n>: <question summary> → <extracted answer> (1 line per answered question, keep brief)\n\n**Ask the applicant:**\n\n> **Q<next>: \"<exact words the intern should say out loud to the applicant>\"**\n\nType the applicant's answer here and I will determine the next question or route.\n\n- Show exactly ONE question — the next unanswered question per the branching logic. Never list all questions.\n- Phrase the question as a **script** the intern reads verbatim. Use plain language the applicant can understand.\n- If a branching sub-question is needed (e.g. Q3A, Q5A), ask that before advancing to the next top-level question.\n\n**OUTPUT C — No information extractable (first message, nothing in the paragraph maps to Part B)**\n\n**Selected Entry:** <ENTRY_ID>\n\n**Part A — Quick briefing** (2-3 sentences max: what this topic covers and what to focus on. No essays.)\n\n**Ask the applicant:**\n\n> **Q1: \"<exact words the intern should say out loud>\"**\n\nType the applicant's answer here and I will determine the next question or route.\n\n**OUTPUT D — No safe match (Unclear)**\n\n**Selected Entry:** Unclear\n\n**What I found:** 1-2 sentences on what the retrieved sources cover and why none match. Cite [filename].\n\n**Ask the applicant these to narrow down:**\n1. <question in plain language>\n2. <question in plain language>\n\nDo NOT include Part B or Part C from unrelated entries.\n\n---\n\nFOLLOW-UP TURNS (intern returns with the applicant's answer)\n\n- Take the new answer, update your map of answered Part B questions.\n- Follow branching logic: can you route now, or do you need more info?\n- **If more info needed:** use OUTPUT B format (show what you know + ask ONE next question).\n- **If ready to route:** use OUTPUT A format (show what you know + give Part C routing as a script).\n- **Never ask a question whose answer you already have** from earlier turns or the original paragraph.\n- Stop asking questions the moment branching logic allows a valid route.\n- If the answer is unclear, ask for minimal clarification on that one question only.\n\n---\n\nHOW TO DECIDE WHICH OUTPUT TO USE\n\n1. First message about a new situation → run auto-extract on the paragraph:\n   - Extracted answers reach a route → OUTPUT A (route immediately)\n   - Extracted some answers, need more → OUTPUT B (show what you know + ask next)\n   - Extracted nothing useful → OUTPUT C (brief Part A + first question)\n   - No matching entry → OUTPUT D (Unclear)\n2. Follow-up message (intern providing applicant's answer) → update answers, then OUTPUT A or B.\n\n---\n\nCRITICAL RULES (do not sway)\n\n- **Every response must be scannable in under 15 seconds.** The applicant is right there.\n- Use Markdown. Use bold headers. Use bullet points. No paragraphs longer than 2 sentences.\n- Base every part (A, B, C) ONLY on the text in the sources. Do not invent questions, routes, or legal conclusions.\n- Keep section headers bold exactly as shown (e.g. `**Selected Entry:**`, `**Ask the applicant:**`, `**Part C — Routing recommendation:**`).\n- Ask exactly ONE question at a time. Stop asking once branching logic allows a route.\n- Do not advance to the next top-level question when a branching sub-question is unresolved.\n- For Part C: apply branching conditions exactly. If logic says \"if Qx = Y then Route Z\", follow literally.\n- Obey the `guardrail` strictly. Do not output a separate \"Guardrails\" section — use it for behavior control only.\n- Do not add generic disclaimers, \"jurisdictional\" caveats, or extra questions (except in Unclear path).\n- Cite every fact with the source name in square brackets. Do not combine sources.\n- If the sources block is empty, say only: \"No relevant guidance was found in the available sources for this situation.\"\n- If sources are non-empty but none match, use OUTPUT D — do not list unrelated triage questions.\n- If a matching entry IS present in sources, you MUST use it (not Unclear).\n- Answer in English only.\nEach image source has the document file name in the top left corner of the image with coordinates (10,10) pixels with format <filename.ext#page=N>,\nand the image figure name is right-aligned in the top right corner of the image.\nThe filename of the actual image is in the top right corner of the image and is in the format <figureN_N.png>.\nEach text source starts in a new line and has the file name followed by colon and the actual information\nAlways include the source document filename for each fact you use in the response in the format: [document_name.ext#page=N].\nIf you are referencing an image, add the image filename in the format: [document_name.ext#page=N(image_name.png)].\n\nPossible citations for current question:  [Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7]  [Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8]  [Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8(figure8_1.png)]  [Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Are interest rates high?\n\n\nSources:\n\nFinancial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7:  This section examines the correlations between stock indices, cryptocurrency prices, and commodity prices, revealing how changes in one market can have ripple effects across the financial ecosystem.### Impact of Macroeconomic Factors   <figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>   Macroeconomic factors such as interest rates, inflation, and GDP growth play a pivotal role in shaping financial markets.\n\nFinancial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8: </figcaption></figure>   Macroeconomic factors such as interest rates, inflation, and GDP growth play a pivotal role in shaping financial markets. This section analyzes how these factors have influenced stock, cryptocurrency, and commodity markets over recent years, providing insights into the complex relationship between the economy and financial market performance.## Future Predictions and Trends   <figure><figcaption>Relative Growth Trends for S&P 500, Bitcoin, and Oil Prices (2024 Indexed to 100)<br>This horizontal bar chart shows prices indexed to 100 for the years 2024 to 2028. It compares the prices of Oil, Bitcoin, and the S&P 500 across these years. In 2024, all three have an index value of 100. From 2025 to 2028, all three generally increase, with Bitcoin consistently having the highest index value, followed closely by the S&P 500 and then Oil. The chart uses grey bars for Oil, orange bars for Bitcoin, and blue bars for the S&P 500.</figcaption></figure>   Based on historical data, current trends, and economic indicators, this section presents predictions for the future of financial markets.\n\nFinancial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2: advanced data analytics to present a clear picture of the complex interplay between different financial markets and their potential trajectories## Introduction to Financial Markets   <figure><figcaption>Global Financial Market Distribution (2023)<br>The pie chart features four categories: Stocks, Bonds, Cryptocurrencies, and Commodities. Stocks take up the largest portion of the chart, represented in blue, accounting for 40%. Bonds are the second largest, shown in orange, making up 25%. Cryptocurrencies are depicted in gray and cover 20% of the chart. Commodities are the smallest segment, shown in yellow, comprising 15%.</figcaption></figure>   The global financial market is a vast and intricate network of exchanges, instruments, and assets, ranging from traditional stocks and bonds to modern cryptocurrencies and commodities. Each segment plays a crucial role in the overall economy, and their interactions can have profound effects on global financial stability. This section provides an overview of these segments and sets the stage for a detailed analysis## Stock Market Overview   <figure><figcaption><br>The image is a line graph titled \"5-Year Trend of the S&P 500 Index."}, {"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8z/C/HgAGgwJ/lK3Q6wAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==", "detail": "auto"}}]}], "props": {"model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "deployment": "test-chatgpt", "token_usage": {"prompt_tokens": 23, "completion_tokens": 896, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "total_tokens": 919}}}], "data_points": {"text": ["Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7:  This section examines the correlations between stock indices, cryptocurrency prices, and commodity prices, revealing how changes in one market can have ripple effects across the financial ecosystem.### Impact of Macroeconomic Factors   <figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>   Macroeconomic factors such as interest rates, inflation, and GDP growth play a pivotal role in shaping financial markets.", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8: </figcaption></figure>   Macroeconomic factors such as interest rates, inflation, and GDP growth play a pivotal role in shaping financial markets. This section analyzes how these factors have influenced stock, cryptocurrency, and commodity markets over recent years, providing insights into the complex relationship between the economy and financial market performance.## Future Predictions and Trends   <figure><figcaption>Relative Growth Trends for S&P 500, Bitcoin, and Oil Prices (2024 Indexed to 100)<br>This horizontal bar chart shows prices indexed to 100 for the years 2024 to 2028. It compares the prices of Oil, Bitcoin, and the S&P 500 across these years. In 2024, all three have an index value of 100. From 2025 to 2028, all three generally increase, with Bitcoin consistently having the highest index value, followed closely by the S&P 500 and then Oil. The chart uses grey bars for Oil, orange bars for Bitcoin, and blue bars for the S&P 500.</figcaption></figure>   Based on historical data, current trends, and economic indicators, this section presents predictions for the future of financial markets.", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2: advanced data analytics to present a clear picture of the complex interplay between different financial markets and their potential trajectories## Introduction to Financial Markets   <figure><figcaption>Global Financial Market Distribution (2023)<br>The pie chart features four categories: Stocks, Bonds, Cryptocurrencies, and Commodities. Stocks take up the largest portion of the chart, represented in blue, accounting for 40%. Bonds are the second largest, shown in orange, making up 25%. Cryptocurrencies are depicted in gray and cover 20% of the chart. Commodities are the smallest segment, shown in yellow, comprising 15%.</figcaption></figure>   The global financial market is a vast and intricate network of exchanges, instruments, and assets, ranging from traditional stocks and bonds to modern cryptocurrencies and commodities. Each segment plays a crucial role in the overall economy, and their interactions can have profound effects on global financial stability. This section provides an overview of these segments and sets the stage for a detailed analysis## Stock Market Overview   <figure><figcaption><br>The image is a line graph titled \"5-Year Trend of the S&P 500 Index."], "images": ["data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8z/C/HgAGgwJ/lK3Q6wAAAABJRU5ErkJggg=="], "citations": ["Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8(figure8_1.png)", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2"], "external_results_metadata": []}, "followup_questions": null}, "session_state": null}
{"delta": {"role": "assistant", "content": "From the provided sources, the impact of interest rates and GDP growth on financial markets can be observed through the line graph. [Financial Market Analysis Report 2023-7.png]"}}
{"delta": {"role": "assistant"}, "context": {"thoughts": [{"title": "Prompt to generate search query", "description": [{"role": "system", "content": "Below is a history of the conversation so far, and a new message from the volunteer. The volunteer is entering the applicant's situation or follow-up answers to be searched in the legal knowledge base.\nYou have access to an Azure AI Search index containing legal guidance, triage questions, and routing information.\nGenerate a search query based on the conversation and the new message. The query should capture the legal issue or situation so that the right Golden Set entry (Part A + Part B) can be retrieved.\nTreat the newest volunteer message as the primary source of truth for topic selection.\nIf the newest message indicates a different legal issue from earlier turns, ignore earlier-turn legal terms entirely and build the query from the newest message only.\nDo not carry over nouns, institutions, statutes, or issue labels from previous turns unless the newest message clearly refers to the same matter.\nIf the newest message looks like a short follow-up answer to a prior triage question (for example \"yes\", \"no\", a date, an amount, or a brief fact update), preserve the same legal topic from recent turns and include topic terms from that ongoing issue in the search query.\nWhen the conversation already has a stable selected Golden Set entry/topic and the newest message is an answer-only update, keep retrieval anchored to that same entry/topic instead of switching domains.\nPrefer terms that match how entries are written in the index: **topic**, **category**, and distinctive phrases similar to **`user_query`** / **`variations`** in the knowledge base. Include a **Golden Set code** (e.g. EMP-01, FAM-03, BKR-02) only when the topic clearly maps to that bucket—codes appear in indexed JSON and filenames such as `EMP-01.json`.\nWhen the volunteer’s wording is colloquial, **carry over their concrete nouns and verbs** (e.g. who, what happened, which institution) into the search query; do not replace everything with generic words.\nUse the correct **legal domain** keywords for the issue (e.g. family law vs employment vs bankruptcy) so the query is not dominated by the wrong area.\nIf the volunteer asks about eligibility or routing through legal aid programs, include relevant program terms (for example legal aid eligibility, PCHI, savings threshold, civil/criminal/family routing, LAB, CLAS, PDO, LASCO, FJSS).\nDo not paste long file paths or irrelevant document titles into the search query. **Golden Set entry codes** (e.g. EMP-01, FAM-03) are allowed when they match the legal issue and improve retrieval.\nDo not include any text inside [] or <<>> in the search query terms.\nDo not include any special characters like '+'.\nAssume all messages are in English.\nWhen uncertain between history and latest message, prefer the latest message and output a concise, neutral query without a Golden Set code.\nIf you cannot generate a search query, return just the number 0.\n\nGenerate search query for: My employer hasn't paid my salary for 3 months. What can I do?\n\nSearch query: Unpaid salary employer not paid wages claim TADM Employment Act\n\nGenerate search query for: Applicant was fired without reason last week.\n\nSearch query: Wrongful dismissal unfair termination TADM Employment Act\n\nGenerate search query for: My ex won't let me see my kids.\n\nSearch query: FAM-03 child access denied visitation custody ex-spouse co-parent Family Law care and control joint custody FJC children\n\nGenerate search query for: I need help dividing my late parent's estate among the siblings.\n\nSearch query: Probate estate administration will intestacy beneficiaries succession Wills\n\n\nGenerate search query for: Are interest rates high?"}], "props": {"model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "deployment": "test-chatgpt", "token_usage": {"prompt_tokens": 23, "completion_tokens": 896, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "total_tokens": 919}}}, {"title": "Search using generated search query", "description": "interest rates", "props": {"use_semantic_captions": false, "use_semantic_ranker": false, "use_query_rewriting": false, "use_azure_search_query_rewriting": false, "top": 3, "filter": null, "use_vector_search": true, "use_text_search": true, "search_text_embeddings": true, "search_image_embeddings": true, "rewrite_fallback_to_user_query": false, "minimum_search_score": 0.0, "minimum_reranker_score": 0.0}}, {"title": "Search results", "description": [{"type": "searchIndex", "id": "file-Financial_Market_Analysis_Report_2023_pdf-46696E616E6369616C204D61726B657420416E616C79736973205265706F727420323032332E706466-page-7", "content": " This\nsection examines the correlations between stock indices, cryptocurrency prices, and commodity prices,\nrevealing how changes in one market can have ripple effects across the financial ecosystem.### Impact of Macroeconomic Factors\n\n\n<figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>\n\n\nMacroeconomic factors such as interest\nrates, inflation, and GDP growth play a\npivotal role in shaping financial markets.", "category": null, "sourcepage": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7", "sourcefile": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf", "oids": null, "groups": null, "captions": [], "score": null, "reranker_score": null, "activity": null, "images": []}, {"type": "searchIndex", "id": "file-Financial_Market_Analysis_Report_2023_pdf-46696E616E6369616C204D61726B657420416E616C79736973205265706F727420323032332E706466-page-8", "content": "</figcaption></figure>\n\n\nMacroeconomic factors such as interest\nrates, inflation, and GDP growth play a\npivotal role in shaping financial markets.\nThis section analyzes how these factors\nhave influenced stock, cryptocurrency,\nand commodity markets over recent\nyears, providing insights into the\ncomplex relationship between the\neconomy and financial market\nperformance.## Future Predictions and Trends\n\n\n<figure><figcaption>Relative Growth Trends for S&P 500, Bitcoin, and Oil Prices (2024 Indexed to 100)<br>This horizontal bar chart shows prices indexed to 100 for the years 2024 to 2028. It compares the prices of Oil, Bitcoin, and the S&P 500 across these years. In 2024, all three have an index value of 100. From 2025 to 2028, all three generally increase, with Bitcoin consistently having the highest index value, followed closely by the S&P 500 and then Oil. The chart uses grey bars for Oil, orange bars for Bitcoin, and blue bars for the S&P 500.</figcaption></figure>\n\n\nBased on historical data, current trends,\nand economic indicators, this section\npresents predictions for the future of\nfinancial markets.", "category": null, "sourcepage": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8", "sourcefile": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf", "oids": null, "groups": null, "captions": [], "score": null, "reranker_score": null, "activity": null, "images": [{"url": "https://sticygqdubf4x6w.blob.core.windows.net/images/Financial%20Market%20Analysis%20Report%202023.pdf/page7/figure8_1.png", "description": "<figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>", "boundingbox": [63.1008, 187.9416, 561.3408000000001, 483.5088]}]}, {"type": "searchIndex", "id": "file-Financial_Market_Analysis_Report_2023_pdf-46696E616E6369616C204D61726B657420416E616C79736973205265706F727420323032332E706466-page-1", "content": "advanced data\nanalytics to present a clear picture of the complex interplay between\ndifferent financial markets and their potential trajectories## Introduction to Financial Markets\n\n\n<figure><figcaption>Global Financial Market Distribution (2023)<br>The pie chart features four categories: Stocks, Bonds, Cryptocurrencies, and Commodities. Stocks take up the largest portion of the chart, represented in blue, accounting for 40%. Bonds are the second largest, shown in orange, making up 25%. Cryptocurrencies are depicted in gray and cover 20% of the chart. Commodities are the smallest segment, shown in yellow, comprising 15%.</figcaption></figure>\n\n\nThe global financial market is a vast and intricate network of\nexchanges, instruments, and assets, ranging from traditional stocks\nand bonds to modern cryptocurrencies and commodities. Each\nsegment plays a crucial role in the overall economy, and their\ninteractions can have profound effects on global financial stability.\nThis section provides an overview of these segments and sets the\nstage for a detailed analysis## Stock Market Overview\n\n\n<figure><figcaption><br>The image is a line graph titled \"5-Year Trend of the S&P 500 Index.", "category": null, "sourcepage": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2", "sourcefile": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf", "oids": null, "groups": null, "captions": [], "score": null, "reranker_score": null, "activity": null, "images": [{"url": "https://sticygqdubf4x6w.blob.core.windows.net/images/Financial%20Market%20Analysis%20Report%202023.pdf/page7/figure8_1.png", "description": "<figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>", "boundingbox": [63.1008, 187.9416, 561.3408000000001, 483.5088]}]}], "props": null}, {"title": "Prompt to generate answer", "description": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a Legal Triage Assistant for Pro Bono SG hotline interns and volunteers. The intern has ZERO legal knowledge — you are their expert guide. A applicant is sitting right in front of the intern, so every response must be SHORT, ACTIONABLE, and SCANNABLE IN UNDER 15 SECONDS. No walls of text. No essays.\n\nYour job: read the intern's description, match it to the right Golden Set entry, figure out what you already know from their paragraph, and tell the intern exactly what to do next — either ask ONE specific follow-up question or route the applicant immediately.\n\n---\n\nSTEP 1 — TOPIC MATCH (mandatory first step)\n\n- Read the intern's description of the applicant's situation.\n- **Scan every source block below** for Golden Set JSON (look for `\"id\":` such as `GEN3-T01`, plus `category`, `topic`, `user_query`, `variations`, and `guardrail`). Do **not** decide from the first snippet or ranking order alone — irrelevant entries may rank higher.\n- **Strong match rule:** If the intern's message clearly paraphrases any entry's `user_query` or `variations`, treat that entry as the primary match when its full JSON appears in the sources.\n- **Pick the one entry** whose legal area and issue match the situation.\n- **Never invent an entry id.** `Selected Entry` must be an id visible in the sources (or Unclear).\n- If **no entry matches after scanning all sources**, use **Unclear** handling (OUTPUT D below).\n\nENTRY CONSISTENCY (apply in every response)\n- Citations may show **sourcefile** `GEN3-T01.json` (one file per Golden Set row). When the basename matches `\"id\"` in the JSON body, treat that as a strong signal.\n- The sources may contain multiple entries. Pick the **single best-matching** one across all chunks.\n- Use ONLY that entry's triage questions, branching logic, routes, and guardrail. Do not merge across entries unless the entry explicitly cross-references another.\n- **Guardrail adherence (non-negotiable):** The `guardrail` field is binding. Never paraphrase away restrictions. If the guardrail forbids something (e.g. interpreting documents, applying law to facts), refuse that part.\n- If no clear match exists after scanning all sources, use Unclear handling.\n\n---\n\nSTEP 2 — AUTO-EXTRACT (the critical innovation)\n\nAfter matching a topic, read the intern's paragraph and check EVERY Part B question from `triage_questions` and `branching_logic` in that entry:\n\n- For each question, determine if the intern's paragraph **already provides the answer** — either explicitly stated or clearly implied.\n- Be conservative: only mark a question as answered if the paragraph **unambiguously** provides the answer. If ambiguous, treat as unanswered.\n- Follow the `branching_logic` with the answered questions to determine how far down the decision tree you can get.\n- Detect cross-references: if the description reveals urgency cues (violence, self-harm, court date within 14 days, homelessness), flag GEN3-T06 concurrently. If vulnerability cues appear (elderly, minor, language barrier, disability), flag GEN3-T13 concurrently.\n\n---\n\nSTEP 3 — RESPOND (use the right output format)\n\n**OUTPUT A — Can route immediately (all necessary questions answered or branching logic reaches a route)**\n\n**Selected Entry:** <ENTRY_ID>\n\n**What I gathered from your description:**\n- Q<n>: <question summary> → <extracted answer> (1 line per answered question)\n\n**Part C — Routing recommendation:** Route <letter> (<name>)\n\nTell the applicant: **\"<exact script the intern reads aloud to the applicant — 2-3 sentences max, plain language, no legal jargon>\"**\n\nNext steps for you (the intern):\n- <1-2 bullet points of what the intern should do, e.g. \"Take down applicant's name and contact\" or \"Transfer to PBSG Staff\">\n\nCite [filename] for every fact.\n\n**OUTPUT B — Need more info (some questions answered, more needed before routing)**\n\n**Selected Entry:** <ENTRY_ID>\n\n**What I gathered from your description:**\n- Q<n>: <question summary> → <extracted answer> (1 line per answered question, keep brief)\n\n**Ask the applicant:**\n\n> **Q<next>: \"<exact words the intern should say out loud to the applicant>\"**\n\nType the applicant's answer here and I will determine the next question or route.\n\n- Show exactly ONE question — the next unanswered question per the branching logic. Never list all questions.\n- Phrase the question as a **script** the intern reads verbatim. Use plain language the applicant can understand.\n- If a branching sub-question is needed (e.g. Q3A, Q5A), ask that before advancing to the next top-level question.\n\n**OUTPUT C — No information extractable (first message, nothing in the paragraph maps to Part B)**\n\n**Selected Entry:** <ENTRY_ID>\n\n**Part A — Quick briefing** (2-3 sentences max: what this topic covers and what to focus on. No essays.)\n\n**Ask the applicant:**\n\n> **Q1: \"<exact words the intern should say out loud>\"**\n\nType the applicant's answer here and I will determine the next question or route.\n\n**OUTPUT D — No safe match (Unclear)**\n\n**Selected Entry:** Unclear\n\n**What I found:** 1-2 sentences on what the retrieved sources cover and why none match. Cite [filename].\n\n**Ask the applicant these to narrow down:**\n1. <question in plain language>\n2. <question in plain language>\n\nDo NOT include Part B or Part C from unrelated entries.\n\n---\n\nFOLLOW-UP TURNS (intern returns with the applicant's answer)\n\n- Take the new answer, update your map of answered Part B questions.\n- Follow branching logic: can you route now, or do you need more info?\n- **If more info needed:** use OUTPUT B format (show what you know + ask ONE next question).\n- **If ready to route:** use OUTPUT A format (show what you know + give Part C routing as a script).\n- **Never ask a question whose answer you already have** from earlier turns or the original paragraph.\n- Stop asking questions the moment branching logic allows a valid route.\n- If the answer is unclear, ask for minimal clarification on that one question only.\n\n---\n\nHOW TO DECIDE WHICH OUTPUT TO USE\n\n1. First message about a new situation → run auto-extract on the paragraph:\n   - Extracted answers reach a route → OUTPUT A (route immediately)\n   - Extracted some answers, need more → OUTPUT B (show what you know + ask next)\n   - Extracted nothing useful → OUTPUT C (brief Part A + first question)\n   - No matching entry → OUTPUT D (Unclear)\n2. Follow-up message (intern providing applicant's answer) → update answers, then OUTPUT A or B.\n\n---\n\nCRITICAL RULES (do not sway)\n\n- **Every response must be scannable in under 15 seconds.** The applicant is right there.\n- Use Markdown. Use bold headers. Use bullet points. No paragraphs longer than 2 sentences.\n- Base every part (A, B, C) ONLY on the text in the sources. Do not invent questions, routes, or legal conclusions.\n- Keep section headers bold exactly as shown (e.g. `**Selected Entry:**`, `**Ask the applicant:**`, `**Part C — Routing recommendation:**`).\n- Ask exactly ONE question at a time. Stop asking once branching logic allows a route.\n- Do not advance to the next top-level question when a branching sub-question is unresolved.\n- For Part C: apply branching conditions exactly. If logic says \"if Qx = Y then Route Z\", follow literally.\n- Obey the `guardrail` strictly. Do not output a separate \"Guardrails\" section — use it for behavior control only.\n- Do not add generic disclaimers, \"jurisdictional\" caveats, or extra questions (except in Unclear path).\n- Cite every fact with the source name in square brackets. Do not combine sources.\n- If the sources block is empty, say only: \"No relevant guidance was found in the available sources for this situation.\"\n- If sources are non-empty but none match, use OUTPUT D — do not list unrelated triage questions.\n- If a matching entry IS present in sources, you MUST use it (not Unclear).\n- Answer in English only.\nEach image source has the document file name in the top left corner of the image with coordinates (10,10) pixels with format <filename.ext#page=N>,\nand the image figure name is right-aligned in the top right corner of the image.\nThe filename of the actual image is in the top right corner of the image and is in the format <figureN_N.png>.\nEach text source starts in a new line and has the file name followed by colon and the actual information\nAlways include the source document filename for each fact you use in the response in the format: [document_name.ext#page=N].\nIf you are referencing an image, add the image filename in the format: [document_name.ext#page=N(image_name.png)].\n\nPossible citations for current question:  [Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7]  [Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8]  [Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8(figure8_1.png)]  [Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Are interest rates high?\n\n\nSources:\n\nFinancial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7:  This section examines the correlations between stock indices, cryptocurrency prices, and commodity prices, revealing how changes in one market can have ripple effects across the financial ecosystem.### Impact of Macroeconomic Factors   <figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>   Macroeconomic factors such as interest rates, inflation, and GDP growth play a pivotal role in shaping financial markets.\n\nFinancial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8: </figcaption></figure>   Macroeconomic factors such as interest rates, inflation, and GDP growth play a pivotal role in shaping financial markets. This section analyzes how these factors have influenced stock, cryptocurrency, and commodity markets over recent years, providing insights into the complex relationship between the economy and financial market performance.## Future Predictions and Trends   <figure><figcaption>Relative Growth Trends for S&P 500, Bitcoin, and Oil Prices (2024 Indexed to 100)<br>This horizontal bar chart shows prices indexed to 100 for the years 2024 to 2028. It compares the prices of Oil, Bitcoin, and the S&P 500 across these years. In 2024, all three have an index value of 100. From 2025 to 2028, all three generally increase, with Bitcoin consistently having the highest index value, followed closely by the S&P 500 and then Oil. The chart uses grey bars for Oil, orange bars for Bitcoin, and blue bars for the S&P 500.</figcaption></figure>   Based on historical data, current trends, and economic indicators, this section presents predictions for the future of financial markets.\n\nFinancial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2: advanced data analytics to present a clear picture of the complex interplay between different financial markets and their potential trajectories## Introduction to Financial Markets   <figure><figcaption>Global Financial Market Distribution (2023)<br>The pie chart features four categories: Stocks, Bonds, Cryptocurrencies, and Commodities. Stocks take up the largest portion of the chart, represented in blue, accounting for 40%. Bonds are the second largest, shown in orange, making up 25%. Cryptocurrencies are depicted in gray and cover 20% of the chart. Commodities are the smallest segment, shown in yellow, comprising 15%.</figcaption></figure>   The global financial market is a vast and intricate network of exchanges, instruments, and assets, ranging from traditional stocks and bonds to modern cryptocurrencies and commodities. Each segment plays a crucial role in the overall economy, and their interactions can have profound effects on global financial stability. This section provides an overview of these segments and sets the stage for a detailed analysis## Stock Market Overview   <figure><figcaption><br>The image is a line graph titled \"5-Year Trend of the S&P 500 Index."}, {"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8z/C/HgAGgwJ/lK3Q6wAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==", "detail": "auto"}}]}], "props": {"model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "deployment": "test-chatgpt", "token_usage": {"prompt_tokens": 23, "completion_tokens": 896, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "total_tokens": 919}}}], "data_points": {"text": ["Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7:  This section examines the correlations between stock indices, cryptocurrency prices, and commodity prices, revealing how changes in one market can have ripple effects across the financial ecosystem.### Impact of Macroeconomic Factors   <figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>   Macroeconomic factors such as interest rates, inflation, and GDP growth play a pivotal role in shaping financial markets.", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8: </figcaption></figure>   Macroeconomic factors such as interest rates, inflation, and GDP growth play a pivotal role in shaping financial markets. This section analyzes how these factors have influenced stock, cryptocurrency, and commodity markets over recent years, providing insights into the complex relationship between the economy and financial market performance.## Future Predictions and Trends   <figure><figcaption>Relative Growth Trends for S&P 500, Bitcoin, and Oil Prices (2024 Indexed to 100)<br>This horizontal bar chart shows prices indexed to 100 for the years 2024 to 2028. It compares the prices of Oil, Bitcoin, and the S&P 500 across these years. In 2024, all three have an index value of 100. From 2025 to 2028, all three generally increase, with Bitcoin consistently having the highest index value, followed closely by the S&P 500 and then Oil. The chart uses grey bars for Oil, orange bars for Bitcoin, and blue bars for the S&P 500.</figcaption></figure>   Based on historical data, current trends, and economic indicators, this section presents predictions for the future of financial markets.", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2: advanced data analytics to present a clear picture of the complex interplay between different financial markets and their potential trajectories## Introduction to Financial Markets   <figure><figcaption>Global Financial Market Distribution (2023)<br>The pie chart features four categories: Stocks, Bonds, Cryptocurrencies, and Commodities. Stocks take up the largest portion of the chart, represented in blue, accounting for 40%. Bonds are the second largest, shown in orange, making up 25%. Cryptocurrencies are depicted in gray and cover 20% of the chart. Commodities are the smallest segment, shown in yellow, comprising 15%.</figcaption></figure>   The global financial market is a vast and intricate network of exchanges, instruments, and assets, ranging from traditional stocks and bonds to modern cryptocurrencies and commodities. Each segment plays a crucial role in the overall economy, and their interactions can have profound effects on global financial stability. This section provides an overview of these segments and sets the stage for a detailed analysis## Stock Market Overview   <figure><figcaption><br>The image is a line graph titled \"5-Year Trend of the S&P 500 Index."], "images": ["data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8z/C/HgAGgwJ/lK3Q6wAAAABJRU5ErkJggg=="], "citations": ["Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8(figure8_1.png)", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2"], "external_results_metadata": []}, "followup_questions": null}, "session_state": null}
